    <article class="article">
      <aside class="key-points" aria-labelledby="key-points-title">
        <h2 id="key-points-title">See It, Hear It, Understand It: Multimedia Vocabulary Learning</h2>
        <dl>
          <dt>Question</dt>
          <dd>When does combining a written word, pronunciation, picture, translation, caption, or video make a second-language word easier to learn and remember?</dd>
          <dt>Answer</dt>
          <dd>The strongest evidence supports <strong>complementary cues</strong>: a visible form, a meaningful image or context, and access to the spoken form can create several routes into the same lexical representation. But “more media” is not a dose-response law. Poorly coordinated channels can split attention or overload working memory.<a class="cite" href="#ref-3">3</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-4">4</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-36">36</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-37">37</a></dd>
          <dt>Best-supported pattern</dt>
          <dd>Pictures are particularly useful for concrete meaning; captions make fleeting speech visible; audio is essential for building aural word-form knowledge; and retrieval or output after exposure converts recognition into more durable knowledge.<a class="cite" href="#ref-11">11</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-21">21</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-39">39</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-45">45</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-50">50</a></dd>
          <dt>Major caution</dt>
          <dd>Do not design for mythical “visual” versus “auditory” learner types. Design for the <strong>information that must be learned</strong>: spelling requires visual form, pronunciation requires sound, concept learning needs meaning, and fluent use requires retrieval in multiple modalities.<a class="cite" href="#ref-30">30</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-39">39</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-48">48</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-60">60</a></dd>
        </dl>
      </aside>

      <section class="section" id="summary">
        <p class="section-kicker">01 · Executive summary</p>
        <h2>Multimedia helps when it builds several useful routes to the same word</h2>
        <p class="lead">A vocabulary item is not one memory. To know a French word such as <em>poire</em>, a learner may need to recognize its written form, recognize /pwaʁ/ in speech, connect both forms to the concept <em>pear</em>, retrieve the word from that concept, and eventually use it in a sentence. Multimedia can help because different media expose different parts of that system.</p>
        <p>The clearest modern synthesis comes from Zhang, Sala and Gobet’s 2025 meta-analysis of multimedia L2 vocabulary learning. Across 2,056 participants, a mixed <strong>L1 translation + picture</strong> condition outperformed L1-only and picture-only learning on immediate tests (Hedges’ g = .334 and .350). Yet the delayed advantage over pictures alone shrank and was only marginal, showing that an immediate multimedia advantage is not the same as durable mastery.<a class="cite" href="#ref-37">37</a></p>
        <p>Gloss research tells a complementary—and more cautionary—story. Ramezanali, Uchihara and Faez found that adding a gloss mode produced a moderate immediate benefit and a smaller delayed benefit.<a class="cite" href="#ref-35">35</a> Mahdi, Mohsen and Almanea’s 2024 second-order meta-analysis synthesized seven meta-analyses representing 136 primary studies and found a medium overall effect of glossing, but <strong>single-mode glosses were more effective than multimode glosses</strong> in that synthesis.<a class="cite" href="#ref-36">36</a> Multimedia therefore has an optimum architecture; it does not improve linearly as components are added.</p>
        <p>For video, the evidence is stronger than the common “watch TV and absorb the language” claim, but also more conditional. Captioned viewing produced a medium vocabulary advantage over uncaptioned viewing in a 2025 meta-analysis of 89 effect sizes from 49 experiments (g = .56).<a class="cite" href="#ref-45">45</a> A broader 2026 meta-analysis of audiovisual input found significant L2 gains across 75 effect sizes from 56 experiments, but heterogeneity was very high and educational/language-focused video tended to outperform entertainment-focused material.<a class="cite" href="#ref-46">46</a></p>

        <div class="finding-grid" aria-label="Review at a glance">
          <div class="finding"><span class="number">60</span><p>Scholarly sources reviewed across cognitive theory, L2 vocabulary, glossing, captions, audiovisual input, and multisensory learning.</p></div>
          <div class="finding"><span class="number">136</span><p>Primary studies represented inside the 2024 second-order gloss meta-analysis alone.</p></div>
          <div class="finding"><span class="number">49</span><p>Independent experiments in the 2025 captioned-viewing vocabulary meta-analysis.</p></div>
        </div>

        <figure class="figure" aria-labelledby="triad-title">
          <p class="figure-number">Figure 1 · Author synthesis</p>
          <h3 class="figure-title" id="triad-title">The three jobs multimedia must do</h3>
          <div class="triad">
            <div class="triad-card"><div class="icon">👁</div><h3>See it</h3><p>Stabilize spelling and orthographic form; connect concrete words to pictures or scenes; make fleeting speech visible through captions.</p></div>
            <div class="triad-card"><div class="icon">◖</div><h3>Hear it</h3><p>Build a phonological representation that can be recognized in real speech and retrieved for pronunciation, not just recognized on a screen.</p></div>
            <div class="triad-card"><div class="icon">↔</div><h3>Understand it</h3><p>Bind the form to a concept through translation, picture, definition, context, examples, and later retrieval across different cues.</p></div>
          </div>
          <figcaption>Conceptual synthesis from dual coding, multimedia learning, bilingual lexical representation, and L2 vocabulary research.<a class="cite" href="#ref-1">1</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-3">3</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-7">7</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-8">8</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-9">9</a></figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="callout">
          <span class="label">Bottom line</span>
          <p><strong>See + hear + understand is a strong design principle only when each element has a distinct instructional job.</strong> A picture should clarify meaning, audio should specify the sound form, text should stabilize orthography or meaning, and practice should later require retrieval without all of those supports.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="method">
        <p class="section-kicker">02 · Review method</p>
        <h2>Evidence was organized by what the learner must encode—not by technology</h2>
        <p>This is a focused narrative literature review with quantitative anchors from recent meta-analyses. The search targeted peer-reviewed work on second-language vocabulary learning with pictures, text, L1 translation, multimedia/hypertext glosses, pronunciation or audio, reading-while-listening, captions, subtitles, audiovisual input, video, gestures, and multisensory enrichment. Foundational cognitive theories were included when they directly explain multimedia design.</p>
        <p>Priority was given to meta-analyses, second-order syntheses, controlled L2 experiments, delayed posttests, and studies that distinguished different dimensions of word knowledge. The review also actively retained null and counterintuitive findings—such as cases where pictures affected response speed but not accuracy, where captions strengthened written more than aural form recognition, and where adding extra gloss modes did not outperform simpler support.<a class="cite" href="#ref-24">24</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-29">29</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-36">36</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-39">39</a></p>

        <details class="method-box">
          <summary>What this review does—and does not—claim</summary>
          <div class="method-content">
            <p><strong>It does claim</strong> that carefully coordinated visual, auditory, and semantic cues can improve several kinds of L2 vocabulary learning and that the effect is supported by multiple independent research traditions.</p>
            <p><strong>It does not claim</strong> that every word needs every modality, that a flashy multimedia interface guarantees learning, that pictures eliminate the need for translation or explanation, or that learners should be sorted into visual/auditory “types.”</p>
            <p><strong>Outcome distinction:</strong> recognition, recall, spelling, aural-form recognition, pronunciation, translation, contextual comprehension, and productive use are different outcomes. An intervention that improves one should not automatically be described as improving all vocabulary knowledge.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <div class="evidence-band">
          <div><strong>Highest-level evidence</strong><span>Recent meta-analyses of glosses, pictures+L1, captioned viewing, and audiovisual input.<a class="cite" href="#ref-33">33</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-35">35</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-36">36</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-37">37</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-40">40</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-45">45</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-46">46</a></span></div>
          <div><strong>Key mechanism</strong><span>Complementary verbal, visual, and auditory cues can create multiple retrieval routes.<a class="cite" href="#ref-1">1</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-2">2</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-6">6</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-7">7</a></span></div>
          <div><strong>Boundary condition</strong><span>Transient audio and excessive simultaneous support can increase processing demands.<a class="cite" href="#ref-4">4</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-5">5</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-48">48</a></span></div>
          <div><strong>Design target</strong><span>Transfer from supported recognition to unsupported retrieval, listening, and production.<a class="cite" href="#ref-9">9</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-39">39</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-50">50</a></span></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="architecture">
        <p class="section-kicker">03 · What multimedia changes</p>
        <h2>A word has several representations, and media expose different ones</h2>
        <p>Dual-coding theory is often summarized as “pictures plus words are better,” but that shorthand is too crude. Paivio’s framework predicts that nonverbal and verbal representations can provide partly independent routes to memory.<a class="cite" href="#ref-1">1</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-2">2</a> Mayer’s multimedia framework adds a second constraint: learners must select, organize, and integrate those representations within limited processing capacity.<a class="cite" href="#ref-3">3</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-4">4</a></p>
        <p>In L2 vocabulary this matters because the target itself is verbal. A printed L2 word and an L1 translation are both verbal-domain cues; a picture is cross-domain semantic support; pronunciation supplies an auditory-phonological form. Zhang et al. explicitly used this distinction to classify L1 input as within-domain, pictures as cross-domain, and L1+picture as mixed-domain input.<a class="cite" href="#ref-37">37</a> Their finding that mixed-domain learning was strongest immediately fits a multiple-route account, but the reduced delayed advantage warns that richer encoding still requires later consolidation and retrieval.</p>

        <figure class="figure" aria-labelledby="loop-title">
          <p class="figure-number">Figure 2 · Author synthesis</p>
          <h3 class="figure-title" id="loop-title">From multimedia exposure to usable vocabulary</h3>
          <div class="cue-loop">
            <div><strong>1 · Perceive form</strong><span>Written spelling and/or spoken word form become discriminable.</span></div>
            <div><strong>2 · Ground meaning</strong><span>Picture, translation, definition or context identifies the intended concept.</span></div>
            <div><strong>3 · Bind the routes</strong><span>Orthographic, phonological and semantic information become associated.</span></div>
            <div><strong>4 · Retrieve without support</strong><span>Later tests, speaking, listening and contextual use strengthen independent access.</span></div>
          </div>
          <figcaption>The first three stages explain encoding; the fourth prevents multimedia from becoming a permanent crutch. Retrieval/output evidence shows that attempting to use vocabulary after audiovisual input improves later recall.<a class="cite" href="#ref-50">50</a></figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>That last step is essential. Rich media can make a learning trial feel extremely fluent: the word is visible, the picture makes the answer obvious, and audio supplies the pronunciation. Carpenter and Olson showed why that feeling should be treated cautiously: pictures can improve learning while also distorting metacognitive judgments.<a class="cite" href="#ref-22">22</a> The instructional goal is therefore not to preserve maximum support; it is to use support to build a representation and then progressively remove it.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="see">
        <p class="section-kicker">04 · See it</p>
        <h2>Pictures are powerful meaning cues—especially when the referent is clear</h2>
        <p>Foundational L2 multimedia research repeatedly found advantages for combining text with pictures. Chun and Plass’s three studies with 160 university German learners found picture+text annotations significantly superior to video+text and text-only annotations for vocabulary performance.<a class="cite" href="#ref-11">11</a> Plass and colleagues similarly found that learners remembered word translations better when both verbal and visual annotations were selected than when only one or neither was selected.<a class="cite" href="#ref-12">12</a> Kost, Foss and Lenzini found that combined textual and pictorial glosses supported incidental vocabulary growth during reading.<a class="cite" href="#ref-13">13</a></p>
        <p>Pictures can be particularly strong for <strong>concrete</strong> words because they can connect an unfamiliar L2 form directly to a referent. Morett found that beginning learners recalled concrete L2 words learned with still images better than words learned with iconic gestures, while L1 glosses alone did not yield the same benefit.<a class="cite" href="#ref-21">21</a> Liu and colleagues add a useful nuance: picture-linked novel Chinese words did not produce higher recognition accuracy than L1-word-linked pairs, but they produced faster correct response times, suggesting more efficient access to the association rather than a universal accuracy gain.<a class="cite" href="#ref-24">24</a></p>

        <figure class="source-figure" aria-labelledby="liu-fig-title">
          <div class="source-figure-head">
            <p class="figure-number">Published Figure A · Liu et al. (2021), Figure 1 · CC BY</p>
            <h3 class="figure-title" id="liu-fig-title">Pictures and L1 words establish different associative learning conditions</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="source-figure-media"><img src="data:image/webp;base64," alt="Experimental task design from Liu et al. 2021 comparing novel Chinese words paired with pictures, Japanese words, and control conditions."></div>
          <figcaption>Original Figure 1 from Liu X, Horinouchi H, Yang Y, et al. (2021), <em>Frontiers in Communication</em> 6:605009, DOI 10.3389/fcomm.2021.605009. Reproduced under the Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) licence. The study found no overall accuracy advantage between picture and L1-word association conditions, but faster correct retrieval in the picture condition.<a class="cite" href="#ref-24">24</a></figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>Pictures also have failure modes. The intended label may be ambiguous—an image can depict <em>glass</em>, <em>cup</em>, <em>drink</em>, <em>water</em>, <em>cold</em>, or <em>transparent</em> simultaneously. Abstract vocabulary is even harder. Strong multimedia design therefore uses pictures as <strong>semantic disambiguators</strong>, not decoration. For abstract items, a short definition, example sentence, contrast, or scene can be more informative than an isolated icon.<a class="cite" href="#ref-9">9</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-19">19</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-27">27</a></p>

        <div class="verdict"><p><strong>Evidence verdict — pictures:</strong> Strong for concrete meaning and often beneficial in combination with verbal support. Not all images are equally diagnostic, and picture superiority should not be generalized automatically to abstract vocabulary, spoken-form knowledge, or productive use.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="hear">
        <p class="section-kicker">05 · Hear it</p>
        <h2>Audio is not decorative pronunciation—it teaches a different representation</h2>
        <p>A learner can “know” the printed word and still fail to recognize it in speech. Sydorenko’s modality experiment makes this dissociation unusually clear. Beginning Russian learners who viewed captions performed better on <strong>written</strong> than aural form recognition, while learners who viewed video with audio but no captions showed the reverse pattern.<a class="cite" href="#ref-39">39</a> In other words, the channel experienced during learning shaped the form later available for recognition.</p>
        <p>This is why a multimedia vocabulary system should not treat the speaker button as an optional flourish. If the target outcome includes listening or pronunciation, the learner needs repeated access to the spoken form. But the evidence also explains why <strong>audio alone</strong> is often harder for beginners: sound disappears. Vidal’s 230-participant study found reading produced larger incidental vocabulary gains than listening at every proficiency level, although the difference narrowed with higher proficiency; reading also produced better one-month retention except at the highest level.<a class="cite" href="#ref-48">48</a> Brown, Waring and Donkaewbua likewise found learning in reading, reading-while-listening, and listening-only modes, but long-term unprompted recall was low overall and weakest in listening-only exposure.<a class="cite" href="#ref-47">47</a></p>
        <p>The practical implication is not “prefer text to audio.” It is <strong>make sound inspectable</strong>. Let learners replay the word, pair it with its visible form early, slow or segment difficult material when necessary, and then test the sound without text. Reading-while-listening and extensive text with audio support can produce substantial vocabulary gains when learners receive enough meaningful exposure.<a class="cite" href="#ref-49">49</a></p>

        <figure class="source-figure" aria-labelledby="andra3-title">
          <div class="source-figure-head">
            <p class="figure-number">Published Figure B · Andrä et al. (2020), Figure 3 · CC BY 4.0</p>
            <h3 class="figure-title" id="andra3-title">An example of coordinated audio, translation, picture/gesture, repetition and spoken output</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="source-figure-media"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Andrä et al. 2020 Figure 3 showing learning trials with spoken English words, German translations, pictures or gestures, and learner repetition."></div>
          <figcaption>Original Figure 3 from Andrä C, Mathias B, Schwager A, Macedonia M, von Kriegstein K. (2020), <em>Educational Psychology Review</em> 32:815–850, DOI 10.1007/s10648-020-09527-z. Reproduced under CC BY 4.0. The design illustrates that the “multimedia” condition was not merely an image: learners heard the L2 word and L1 translation, saw an enrichment cue, heard the L2 word again, and then produced the word.<a class="cite" href="#ref-57">57</a></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="combine">
        <p class="section-kicker">06 · See + hear</p>
        <h2>Captions can bind speech to spelling, but they also bias what gets learned</h2>
        <p>Captions are one of the most consistently supported multimedia aids in L2 research. Montero Perez, Van Den Noortgate and Desmet’s 2013 meta-analysis of 18 studies found large benefits of captioned video for listening comprehension and vocabulary acquisition.<a class="cite" href="#ref-40">40</a> A newer and more specifically vocabulary-focused synthesis by Kurokawa, Hein and Uchihara aggregated 89 effect sizes from 49 experiments and estimated a medium captioning advantage over uncaptioned viewing (g=.56).<a class="cite" href="#ref-45">45</a></p>
        <p>What captions do is not mysterious: they turn a transient acoustic stream into a stable orthographic sequence. This can help learners segment speech and map sound to known spellings. But Sydorenko’s findings show the trade-off: caption exposure can preferentially strengthen written form recognition.<a class="cite" href="#ref-39">39</a> A system that always leaves the word visible may therefore create learners who recognize <em>beaucoup</em> instantly on screen but still miss it in connected speech.</p>
        <p>Video itself can also teach words incidentally. Peters and Webb demonstrated meaning recall and recognition after viewing a full-length L2 television program, with frequency, cognateness and prior vocabulary knowledge affecting acquisition.<a class="cite" href="#ref-43">43</a> Peters later showed that meaningful imagery and on-screen text can make different contributions to what learners pick up from audiovisual input.<a class="cite" href="#ref-44">44</a> The newest broad synthesis by Sutton and Webb confirms that audiovisual input produces language gains, but with 86.8% heterogeneity: media type, learner population, study design and other variables matter greatly.<a class="cite" href="#ref-46">46</a></p>

        <figure class="figure" aria-labelledby="effect-title">
          <p class="figure-number">Figure 3 · Quantitative evidence map</p>
          <h3 class="figure-title" id="effect-title">Selected meta-analytic estimates relevant to multimedia vocabulary</h3>
          <div class="effect-chart">
            <div class="effect-row"><div class="effect-label">Captioned vs uncaptioned viewing</div><div class="effect-track"><div class="effect-fill" style="width:62%"></div></div><div class="effect-value">g=.56</div></div>
            <div class="effect-row"><div class="effect-label">Adding a gloss mode · immediate</div><div class="effect-track"><div class="effect-fill" style="width:51%"></div></div><div class="effect-value">g≈.46</div></div>
            <div class="effect-row"><div class="effect-label">Adding a gloss mode · delayed</div><div class="effect-track"><div class="effect-fill" style="width:31%"></div></div><div class="effect-value">g≈.28</div></div>
            <div class="effect-row"><div class="effect-label">L1 + picture vs L1 only · immediate</div><div class="effect-track"><div class="effect-fill" style="width:37%"></div></div><div class="effect-value">g=.334</div></div>
            <div class="effect-row"><div class="effect-label">L1 + picture vs picture only · immediate</div><div class="effect-track"><div class="effect-fill" style="width:39%"></div></div><div class="effect-value">g=.350</div></div>
          </div>
          <p class="chart-note">These bars visualize effect-size magnitudes reported by different meta-analyses and <strong>must not be treated as a head-to-head ranking</strong>: the contrasts, outcome measures, samples and research designs differ.<a class="cite" href="#ref-35">35</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-37">37</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-45">45</a></p>
          <figcaption>Source estimates: Ramezanali et al. 2021; Zhang, Sala &amp; Gobet 2025; Kurokawa, Hein &amp; Uchihara 2025.<a class="cite" href="#ref-35">35</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-37">37</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-45">45</a></figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>A particularly important design study is Nguyen and Boers’s TED Talk experiment. Learners who watched, <strong>retold the content orally</strong>, and then watched again recalled more vocabulary than learners who simply watched twice; words learners attempted to use were especially likely to be retained.<a class="cite" href="#ref-50">50</a> This suggests that the best multimedia sequence is not “play the media again.” It is exposure → effortful reconstruction → re-exposure.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="understand">
        <p class="section-kicker">07 · Understand it</p>
        <h2>Meaning support works best when it is precise, fast, and removable</h2>
        <p>Multimedia glossing research asks a practical question: when a learner meets an unknown word inside a text or video, what support should appear? The broad answer is that glosses work. Abraham’s meta-analysis found a large effect on incidental vocabulary learning.<a class="cite" href="#ref-31">31</a> Yanagisawa, Webb and Uchihara’s much larger synthesis of 42 studies found immediate learning of 45.3% of glossed target words versus 26.6% without glosses, and delayed learning of 33.4% versus 19.8%.<a class="cite" href="#ref-33">33</a></p>
        <p>Yet support format is consequential. Zhang and Ma found textual glossing had a medium, persistent vocabulary effect, with location and mode influencing outcomes.<a class="cite" href="#ref-34">34</a> Mahdi and colleagues’ second-order synthesis found that beginners benefited particularly strongly, recognition tests yielded larger effects, and single-mode glosses outperformed multimode glosses overall.<a class="cite" href="#ref-36">36</a> That result prevents a simplistic conclusion that every gloss should contain word + translation + image + audio + animation.</p>
        <p>Zhang, Sala and Gobet’s 2025 meta-analysis helps reconcile the literature. For intentional vocabulary learning, <strong>L1 translation + picture</strong> was better immediately than either component alone.<a class="cite" href="#ref-37">37</a> This is compatible with Mahdi et al.’s result because the two syntheses ask different questions and pool different interventions. The lesson is architectural: an L1 translation and a picture often carry complementary semantic information; three or four extra elements may introduce redundancy and distraction.</p>

        <div class="mechanisms">
          <div class="mechanism"><span class="index">01</span><h3>Translation gives precision</h3><p>An L1 equivalent can resolve meaning quickly and reduce ambiguity for beginners, especially for words difficult to depict.<a class="cite" href="#ref-7">7</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-8">8</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-37">37</a></p></div>
          <div class="mechanism"><span class="index">02</span><h3>Picture gives conceptual grounding</h3><p>A relevant image can create a nonverbal route to the concept and may speed access to the association.<a class="cite" href="#ref-21">21</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-24">24</a></p></div>
          <div class="mechanism"><span class="index">03</span><h3>Context gives boundaries of use</h3><p>Sentences, scenes and collocations help learners distinguish senses and learn how a word behaves, moving beyond a one-word translation.<a class="cite" href="#ref-9">9</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-10">10</a></p></div>
          <div class="mechanism"><span class="index">04</span><h3>Retrieval makes meaning independent</h3><p>Once the association exists, learners need opportunities to retrieve the word or meaning without the original cue set.<a class="cite" href="#ref-50">50</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-56">56</a></p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="overload">
        <p class="section-kicker">08 · When multimedia backfires</p>
        <h2>More channels can increase learning—or increase competition for attention</h2>
        <p>The multimedia principle is often misused as an instruction to maximize sensory stimulation. Cognitive-load theory predicts the opposite when information is redundant, poorly synchronized, or irrelevant.<a class="cite" href="#ref-4">4</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-5">5</a> If a learner must read a long definition, inspect a complex illustration, listen to a sentence, watch an animation, and process a translation simultaneously, the interface may consume the very resources needed to encode the word.</p>
        <p>The L2 evidence contains several warnings. Mahdi et al.’s second-order meta-analysis found single-mode glosses more effective than multimode glosses overall.<a class="cite" href="#ref-36">36</a> Çakmak and Erçetin did not find a simple vocabulary advantage from adding pictorial and textual modes in mobile-assisted listening.<a class="cite" href="#ref-29">29</a> Liu et al. found faster retrieval with pictures but no accuracy advantage over L1-word associations.<a class="cite" href="#ref-24">24</a> Chun and Plass found a static picture+text condition superior to video+text for their target words, showing that “richer” dynamic media need not be better.<a class="cite" href="#ref-11">11</a></p>

        <div class="limitations">
          <h3>Five common design errors</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Decorative images:</strong> an attractive visual that does not uniquely identify the target meaning adds load without useful information.</li>
            <li><strong>Permanent captions:</strong> text can support early segmentation but prevent the learner from discovering whether the sound form is independently recognizable.</li>
            <li><strong>Everything at once:</strong> simultaneous picture, waveform, phonetic spelling, translation, definition and example can split attention.</li>
            <li><strong>Recognition-only success:</strong> choosing the correct picture from four options is easier than producing the word or recognizing it in natural speech.</li>
            <li><strong>Learning-style matching:</strong> assigning audio to “auditory learners” and pictures to “visual learners” confuses preference with what the task requires.<a class="cite" href="#ref-30">30</a></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>Multimedia is therefore best treated as <strong>scaffolding</strong>. Early trials can be richly supported; later trials should strip supports strategically. If pronunciation is the objective, remove spelling and test sound-to-meaning. If productive vocabulary is the objective, show a picture or concept and require the word. If spelling is the objective, play the sound and require written production.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="design">
        <p class="section-kicker">09 · Design implications</p>
        <h2>A research-informed vocabulary interaction can be simple</h2>

        <div class="principles-grid">
          <div class="principle"><strong>1 · One clear referent</strong><p>For concrete nouns, use an uncluttered picture that makes the intended concept obvious. If ambiguity remains, add a short translation or label.</p></div>
          <div class="principle"><strong>2 · Native-quality audio</strong><p>Make pronunciation replayable and easy to isolate. The learner must hear the actual form that later appears in speech.</p></div>
          <div class="principle"><strong>3 · Visible spelling, then fading</strong><p>Pair sound with orthography early, then include listening-only trials so caption dependence does not become permanent.</p></div>
          <div class="principle"><strong>4 · Translation is a tool, not a failure</strong><p>For beginners, a concise L1 equivalent can provide precision quickly. Use context and examples to broaden semantic knowledge later.</p></div>
          <div class="principle"><strong>5 · Retrieval after exposure</strong><p>After the learner sees/hears the word, hide the answer and ask for meaning, sound recognition, or production. Rewatching alone is weaker than output between viewings.<a class="cite" href="#ref-50">50</a></p></div>
          <div class="principle"><strong>6 · Mix test directions</strong><p>Use L2→meaning, meaning/picture→L2, audio→meaning, spelling→audio discrimination, and contextual choice. Different tests probe different lexical links.</p></div>
          <div class="principle"><strong>7 · Use scenes for abstract words</strong><p>Words such as <em>pourtant</em>, <em>fierté</em> or <em>justice</em> often need a micro-context, contrast or short scenario rather than an arbitrary icon.</p></div>
          <div class="principle"><strong>8 · Recycle across time and contexts</strong><p>Multimedia encoding is an entry point, not the full retention system. Repetition across episodes, texts and tasks remains essential.<a class="cite" href="#ref-43">43</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-49">49</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-51">51</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-56">56</a></p></div>
        </div>

        <figure class="source-figure" aria-labelledby="andra6-title">
          <div class="source-figure-head">
