        <div class="implications">
          <h3>One defensible interaction sequence</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Orient:</strong> show the French word, a clear picture or micro-scene, and a concise English meaning.</li>
            <li><strong>Hear:</strong> play the French pronunciation while the written form is visible.</li>
            <li><strong>Notice:</strong> optionally replay, highlight a relevant sound-spelling pattern, or place the word in one short sentence.</li>
            <li><strong>Retrieve meaning:</strong> hide the English and ask the learner to identify the meaning from the French word or audio.</li>
            <li><strong>Retrieve form:</strong> show the picture/meaning and ask for the French word; accept speech or typing depending on the goal.</li>
            <li><strong>Remove captions:</strong> include listening-only recognition so aural knowledge develops independently.</li>
            <li><strong>Re-encounter later:</strong> return to the word in a different picture, sentence, speaker, or task rather than repeating one identical card.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="practice-note">This sequence is a synthesis of the literature, not a direct protocol tested as a single package. Its components are individually supported, but the complete sequence should be validated empirically.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="limits">
        <p class="section-kicker">10 · Limits &amp; research gaps</p>
        <h2>The field knows more about recognizing written meanings than about fluent spoken use</h2>
        <p>A large share of multimedia vocabulary research uses immediate receptive tests. Mahdi et al. found recognition tests generated larger effects than other outcome types.<a class="cite" href="#ref-36">36</a> This matters because a product can appear highly successful if learners only choose the right definition immediately after seeing a picture-rich gloss.</p>
        <p>Spoken word-form learning remains comparatively underrepresented. The literature on pictures and L1 translations is dominated by written forms, while listening research shows that orthographic and phonological knowledge can dissociate.<a class="cite" href="#ref-26">26</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-39">39</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-48">48</a> Future studies should therefore test aural form recognition, pronunciation, meaning recall from audio, spontaneous production, and delayed transfer to natural speech.</p>
        <p>Age and proficiency also require better evidence. The 2026 audiovisual meta-analysis found no statistically reliable age moderation overall, but only a small number of young-learner studies were available and estimates were imprecise.<a class="cite" href="#ref-46">46</a> Direct multisensory experiments show developmental differences: pictures and gestures were similarly beneficial for 8- and 12-year-olds, whereas gestures became more advantageous in older adolescents and adults.<a class="cite" href="#ref-57">57</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-59">59</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-60">60</a></p>
        <p>Finally, multimedia is not separable from memory design. A one-time perfect encoding event will still fade. Repetition, spacing, retrieval and contextual variation remain necessary to convert a richly supported first encounter into accessible language knowledge.<a class="cite" href="#ref-9">9</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-10">10</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-50">50</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-56">56</a></p>

        <div class="limitations">
          <h3>Research questions worth testing next</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Does picture + audio + concise translation outperform picture + translation when delayed <strong>aural</strong> recall is the primary outcome?</li>
            <li>When should spelling be faded so captions scaffold listening without creating caption dependence?</li>
            <li>Do photographic, illustrated and context-scene visuals differ for concrete versus abstract French vocabulary?</li>
            <li>How much does learner-generated retrieval after a multimedia card add beyond simply replaying the card?</li>
            <li>Can adaptive systems remove modalities once a learner demonstrates independent access to that lexical route?</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="takeaway" id="conclusion">
        <p class="section-kicker">Conclusion</p>
        <h2>See it. Hear it. Understand it. Then make the learner retrieve it.</h2>
        <p>Multimedia vocabulary learning has a strong scientific basis when the media are <strong>instructionally complementary</strong>. Pictures can ground meaning, written forms can stabilize spelling and segment speech, audio can establish phonological representations, translations and context can disambiguate concepts, and captions can connect the acoustic stream to orthography. But the evidence also rejects “more is always better.” The most effective system is not the one with the greatest number of stimuli on screen; it is the one that gives each cue a job, coordinates those cues, and then removes them so the learner can retrieve the word from sound, meaning, text and context independently.</p>
      </section>

