<tr><td>37</td><td><span class="evidence-tag">Meta-analysis</span></td><td>Zhang, C., Sala, G., &amp; Gobet, F. (2025). Effectiveness of L1 and pictures in multimedia conditions on learning second-language vocabulary: A meta-analysis. Educational Research Review, 47, 100681.</td><td>Across 2,056 participants, L1+picture input outperformed L1-only and picture-only conditions on immediate tests (g=.334 and .350 respectively); the delayed advantage over pictures alone was smaller and only marginal.</td></tr>
<tr><td>38</td><td><span class="evidence-tag">Captions &amp; video</span></td><td>Winke, P., Gass, S., &amp; Sydorenko, T. (2010). The effects of captioning videos used for foreign language listening activities. Language Learning &amp; Technology, 14(1), 65–86.</td><td>Captions generally improved comprehension and vocabulary-related performance across several languages, while learners’ use of captions changed with proficiency and task demands.</td></tr>
<tr><td>39</td><td><span class="evidence-tag">Captions &amp; video</span></td><td>Sydorenko, T. (2010). Modality of input and vocabulary acquisition. Language Learning &amp; Technology, 14(2), 50–73.</td><td>Caption groups learned written word forms better than aural forms, while audio-without-captions produced the reverse pattern: the modality practiced strongly influenced the modality later remembered.</td></tr>
<tr><td>40</td><td><span class="evidence-tag">Meta-analysis</span></td><td>Montero Perez, M., Van Den Noortgate, W., &amp; Desmet, P. (2013). Captioned video for L2 listening and vocabulary learning: A meta-analysis. System, 41(3), 720–739.</td><td>Across 18 studies, captioned video showed large positive effects for listening comprehension and vocabulary acquisition; test type moderated listening effects, while proficiency did not clearly moderate vocabulary effects.</td></tr>
<tr><td>41</td><td><span class="evidence-tag">Captions &amp; video</span></td><td>Montero Perez, M., Peters, E., Clarebout, G., &amp; Desmet, P. (2014). Effects of captioning on video comprehension and incidental vocabulary learning. Language Learning &amp; Technology, 18(1), 118–141.</td><td>Full and keyword-based caption conditions supported vocabulary learning from repeated French video viewing, but different caption formats did not benefit every outcome equally.</td></tr>
<tr><td>42</td><td><span class="evidence-tag">Captions &amp; video</span></td><td>Peters, E., Heynen, E., &amp; Puimège, E. (2016). Learning vocabulary through audiovisual input: The differential effect of L1 subtitles and captions. System, 63, 134–148.</td><td>Both L1 subtitles and L2 captions can increase vocabulary learning from video compared with unsupported viewing, but their relative usefulness depends on proficiency and which aspect of word knowledge is measured.</td></tr>
<tr><td>43</td><td><span class="evidence-tag">Captions &amp; video</span></td><td>Peters, E., &amp; Webb, S. (2018). Incidental vocabulary acquisition through viewing L2 television and factors that affect learning. Studies in Second Language Acquisition, 40(3), 551–577.</td><td>Viewing a full-length L2 television program produced incidental gains in meaning recall and recognition; occurrence frequency, prior vocabulary knowledge, and cognateness influenced learning.</td></tr>
<tr><td>44</td><td><span class="evidence-tag">Captions &amp; video</span></td><td>Peters, E. (2019). The effect of imagery and on-screen text on foreign language vocabulary learning from audiovisual input. TESOL Quarterly, 53(4), 1008–1032.</td><td>Words are more learnable from audiovisual input when imagery makes their meanings inferable and when on-screen text makes their forms salient; imageability and captions can therefore contribute through different routes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>45</td><td><span class="evidence-tag">Meta-analysis</span></td><td>Kurokawa, S., Hein, A. M., &amp; Uchihara, T. (2025). Incidental vocabulary acquisition through captioned viewing: A meta-analysis. Language Learning, 75(4), 939–987.</td><td>Across 89 effect sizes from 49 experiments, captions produced a medium vocabulary benefit (g=.56); instructional level, video target audience, and vocabulary pretesting moderated the effect.</td></tr>
<tr><td>46</td><td><span class="evidence-tag">Meta-analysis</span></td><td>Sutton, D., &amp; Webb, S. (2026). The effects of audiovisual input on second language learning: A meta-analysis. Studies in Second Language Acquisition, 48(2), 571–595.</td><td>Across 75 effect sizes from 56 experiments (n=1,954), audiovisual viewing produced significant L2 gains (g=.89) with high heterogeneity; educational and language-focused videos tended to outperform entertainment-focused video.</td></tr>
<tr><td>47</td><td><span class="evidence-tag">Reading &amp; listening</span></td><td>Brown, R., Waring, R., &amp; Donkaewbua, S. (2008). Incidental vocabulary acquisition from reading, reading-while-listening, and listening to stories. Reading in a Foreign Language, 20(2), 136–163.</td><td>Words could be learned incidentally in all three input modes, but long-term unprompted recall was low, especially for listening-only; repeated occurrences made learning and retention more likely.</td></tr>
<tr><td>48</td><td><span class="evidence-tag">Reading &amp; listening</span></td><td>Vidal, K. (2011). A comparison of the effects of reading and listening on incidental vocabulary acquisition. Language Learning, 61(1), 219–258.</td><td>Reading produced larger vocabulary gains than listening at all four proficiency levels and better one-month retention except among the most proficient learners, where the gap disappeared.</td></tr>
<tr><td>49</td><td><span class="evidence-tag">Reading &amp; listening</span></td><td>Webb, S., &amp; Chang, A. C.-S. (2015). Second language vocabulary learning through extensive reading with audio support: How do frequency and distribution of occurrence affect learning? Language Teaching Research, 19(6), 667–686.</td><td>Reading multiple graded texts with audio support yielded substantial vocabulary gains that remained sizable three months later; simple encounter frequency did not explain learning on its own.</td></tr>
<tr><td>50</td><td><span class="evidence-tag">Retrieval &amp; video</span></td><td>Nguyen, C.-D., &amp; Boers, F. (2019). The effect of content retelling on vocabulary uptake from a TED Talk. TESOL Quarterly, 53(1), 5–29.</td><td>Learners who watched, orally retold, and watched again recalled more word meanings immediately and later than learners who simply watched twice; attempting to use a word predicted stronger retention.</td></tr>
